// An assertion in EXPRESSION position, and an assertion whose failure leaves an
// ordinary `fn` instead of aborting a test case.
//
// The failure a lowered assertion builds has to match what `nomi test` reports.
// For the `fn` boundary that means the same eight-field Nomi value
// `testing.check` goes through (rt's AssertionFailure.NomiFailure), read here by
// field selector instead of by a renderer. The VM's output is checked against
// the recorded golden.
//
// The file EXITS 1 on purpose. Two of its cases fail, and their reports are the
// part of this fixture that is hardest to get right: a failing assertion's
// rendered `values:` and `defined as:` rows are observable behaviour, and a
// lowering that judged correctly while rendering differently would pass every
// self-consistency check and diverge from `nomi test`. Both halves — passing and
// failing — because a lowering that always exited early would satisfy the
// failing half alone.
//
// What each case discriminates:
//
//   - `a failing assertion in a plain fn is that fn's Err`: the boundary. There
//     is one exit for a failed assertion and it carries `Result.Err` of
//     std/assertions' `AssertionFailure`; outside a test that value IS the
//     function's result, so `line`, `keyword`, `expression` and `reason` are read
//     off it. The LINE is the assertion's own and not the caller's.
//   - `a failing pattern assertion in a plain fn is that fn's Err` — the same
//     boundary reached from `assert pattern = value`, which is a different
//     lowering with the same exit. Its `reason` is "pattern did not match", so a
//     lowering that reused the Bool reason is caught.
//   - `a passing assertion in a plain fn falls through to Ok` — the branch NOT
//     taken. Without it a guard that always propagated would pass the two above.
//   - `an assertion is an expression whose value is its subject` — `assert` and
//     `refute` as a binding's initializer. The assertion returns
//     `success`, so the value is the SUBJECT and not Unit; `refute` binds the
//     False it refuted.
//   - `a failing assertion renders its operands` — the `values:` rows, in the
//     order they were recorded, for a comparison whose two sides are both names.
//   - `a failing assertion on a name an assertion defined shows its source` — the
//     `defined as:` row for a binding whose initializer is itself an assertion.
//     The builder bakes that text in at build time (gen.binding's debugExpr) and
//     the formatter renders the same AST, so this is the one
//     case where the expression-position lowering has to agree with the formatter
//     about how `assert 1 == 1` reads.
import std/assertions.AssertionFailure

fn checked_equal(a: Int, b: Int): Result<Unit, AssertionFailure> {
    assert a == b
    Ok(Unit)
}

fn first_pair(xs: List<Int>): Result<Unit, AssertionFailure> {
    assert [_, _] = xs
    Ok(Unit)
}

test "a failing assertion in a plain fn is that fn's Err" {
    assert Err(failure) = checked_equal(1, 2)

    assert failure.line == 46
    assert failure.keyword == "assert"
    assert failure.expression == "a == b"
    assert failure.reason == "assertion failed"
}

test "a failing pattern assertion in a plain fn is that fn's Err" {
    assert Err(failure) = first_pair([1])

    assert failure.line == 51
    assert failure.keyword == "assert"
    assert failure.reason == "pattern did not match"
}

test "a passing assertion in a plain fn falls through to Ok" {
    assert Ok(_) = checked_equal(3, 3)
    assert Ok(_) = first_pair([1, 2])
}

test "an assertion is an expression whose value is its subject" {
    truth = assert 1 == 1
    falsity = refute 2 == 1

    assert truth == True
    assert falsity == False
}

test "a failing assertion renders its operands" {
    left = 41
    right = 42

    assert left == right
}

test "a failing assertion on a name an assertion defined shows its source" {
    truth = assert 1 == 1

    refute truth
}
